<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The logging configuration shared by the three services that depend on shared.

  It lives here rather than in each module for the same reason as SecurityConfig and
  GlobalExceptionHandler: the log format is a contract between services, and three copies
  drift apart. The gateway has a copy of its own because it does NOT depend on shared -
  shared brings Tomcat, which has no place in a WebFlux application.

  ============================================================================
  WHY IT EXISTS: the request id was invisible
  ============================================================================

  RequestCorrelationFilter had always been putting the id into the MDC, but without a pattern
  to print it that value appeared nowhere. Meanwhile 127 calls out of 322 were passing it
  around by hand inside the message - and the other 195 had no way of being traced back to a
  request at all.

  %X{requestId} puts it on EVERY line, including Spring's, Hibernate's and Flyway's, without
  anybody having to remember it.

  ============================================================================
  WHICH LEVEL FOR WHICH CASE
  ============================================================================

  There is one rule: THE LEVEL SAYS WHO HAS TO DO SOMETHING, not how serious it is.

  DEBUG  The narration of what a request is doing, step by step.
         Nobody has to do anything: it is for whoever is watching right now.
         OFF in production (logging.level.com.classroom=INFO).
         For example: "START login", "room 3 is free over the requested period".

  INFO   Something has CHANGED and it still matters tomorrow: a user created, a login
         succeeded, a booking cancelled, the first administrator generated.
         Nobody has to act now, but somebody may have to reconstruct it later.
         ON in production. It is the record of what happened.

  WARN   The request was REFUSED, and the refusal says something: an access denied, a
         conflict, too many attempts, an unreachable downstream service, a body that does
         not honour the contract.
         The system did the right thing: nobody needs to act on a single line, but many
         identical lines are a signal.
         It corresponds to 400, 401, 403, 409, 429 and 503.

  ERROR  NOBODY KNOWS WHAT HAPPENED, and somebody has to look.
         It is the only level that asks for a human, and that is why it is rare in the code:
         14 calls out of 322. It always goes with the stack trace.
         It corresponds to 500, that is, to handleGeneric in GlobalExceptionHandler.

  The practical consequence of this scale: an ERROR in the production logs is a fact, not
  noise. If ERROR were used for expected refusals too, finding the real failures would mean
  filtering them out - and at that point you may as well not have the level.

  TRACE is not used: when that much detail is needed, what is needed is a debugger.
-->
<configuration>

    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

    <!--
      requestId padded to 12 characters (REQ_XXXXXXXX occupies exactly 12), so the lines stay
      in columns and can be read at a glance.

      A MINIMUM width and not a maximum: %-12X pads, %-12.12X would TRUNCATE - and Logback
      truncates from the left. A longer id, such as the one a client can send in the
      X-Request-Id header, would arrive mutilated and two different values could come out
      looking identical. A crooked column beats an id you cannot trust.

      The dash marks the lines born outside a request: startup, scheduled jobs, message
      consumption before the id is applied.

      %wEx and not %ex: it is Spring Boot's converter, which shortens stack traces by
      dropping the framework frames and keeping the project's.
    -->
    <property name="LOG_PATTERN"
              value="%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS} %-5level %-12X{requestId:--} %-34.34logger{0} : %msg%n%wEx"/>

    <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder>
            <pattern>${LOG_PATTERN}</pattern>
            <charset>UTF-8</charset>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!--
      The file exists ONLY in development, and ONLY outside a container. In a container you
      would be writing inside the image, where nobody reads it and nobody rotates it: there
      the logs go to stdout and Docker collects them. The rotation policy sits here, next to
      the file it applies to - it used to be declared in the prod profile, which has no file.

      "dev &amp; !docker" and not plain "dev": the path is relative, so inside the container it
      resolves somewhere that is not writable and the service dies at startup. That failure
      is why docker-compose.yml used to avoid the dev profile altogether and flip individual
      flags instead. Compose now activates "dev,docker", which selects development behaviour
      everywhere except this appender.
    -->
    <springProfile name="dev &amp; !docker">
        <appender name="FILE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender">
            <file>logs/application.log</file>
            <rollingPolicy class="ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy">
                <fileNamePattern>logs/application.%d{yyyy-MM-dd}.%i.log.gz</fileNamePattern>
                <maxFileSize>10MB</maxFileSize>
                <maxHistory>30</maxHistory>
                <totalSizeCap>500MB</totalSizeCap>
            </rollingPolicy>
            <encoder>
                <pattern>${LOG_PATTERN}</pattern>
                <charset>UTF-8</charset>
            </encoder>
        </appender>

        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
            <appender-ref ref="FILE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

    <!-- Everything else - production, and development inside a container - logs to stdout. -->
    <springProfile name="!dev | docker">
        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

</configuration>
